---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import GradeReferenceTable from '../components/GradeReferenceTable.astro';
import { gradeReference } from '../data/grade-reference';
const title = 'GPA Scale Chart: 4.0 Grades, Percentages & Weighted GPA';
const description = 'Compare letter grades and grade points on a 4.0 GPA scale, see an example percentage chart, and understand A-minus, college and weighted GPA differences.';
const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Article', headline: title, description,
  mainEntityOfPage: 'https://gpacalculatorfree.com/gpa-scale/',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'GPA Calculator Free', url: 'https://gpacalculatorfree.com/about/' },
  dateModified: '2026-11-23',
};
const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is a 3.0 GPA in letter grades?', a: 'On the scale shown here, a B earns 3.0 grade points. A 3.0 overall GPA can also come from a mix of higher and lower grades; it does not mean every course was a B.' },
  { q: 'Is a 4.0 GPA always 100%?', a: 'No. A 4.0 GPA describes grade points, not a percentage average. Different percentage marks can earn an A, and schools set their own cutoffs.' },
  { q: 'Do P and W grades count toward GPA?', a: 'Pass and withdrawal marks are usually excluded from both grade points and GPA credits. A failing grade usually counts as zero points while its credits stay in the denominator. Check your school’s policy, especially for incomplete and repeated courses.' },
];
---
<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical="/gpa-scale" ogType="article" schema={schema}>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4">
      <nav class="breadcrumb mb-4" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">GPA Scale</span></nav>
      <h1 class="mb-3">GPA Scale Chart</h1>
      <p class="text-lg mb-4">On a 4.0 GPA scale, <strong>A = 4.0, B = 3.0, C = 2.0, D = 1.0 and F = 0.0</strong>. Plus/minus grades fill the gaps. Your school's rules determine the exact points and percentage cutoffs.</p>
      <p class="text-sm">By <a href="/about/">GPA Calculator Free</a> · Updated <time datetime="2026-11-23">November 23, 2026</time></p>
      <nav class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mt-4" aria-label="On this page">
        {[["4.0 chart", "#standard-scale"], ["A-minus GPA", "#a-minus"], ["College scales", "#college-scale"], ["Weighted GPA", "#weighted-scale"], ["Sources", "#sources"]].map(([label, href]) => <a class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm" href={href}>{label}</a>)}
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-8 prose">
    <section aria-labelledby="standard-scale">
      <h2 id="standard-scale">4.0 GPA scale: letters, points and example percentages</h2>
      <p>The grade-point column below matches the undergraduate scale published by the <a href="https://registrar.umd.edu/grades-records/grades/gpa-calculation">University of Maryland registrar</a>. The percentage bands are this site's example convention, used in our calculator percentage modes. They are <strong>not Maryland's published percentage cutoffs or a nationwide standard</strong>.</p>
      <p>For decimal marks, “83–&lt;87%” means at least 83% but less than 87%. It includes 86.5%. Follow your course's rounding policy before converting a mark.</p>
      <GradeReferenceTable />
      <p>Have a number such as 75, 86 or 103? Read <a href="/percentage-to-gpa/">how to convert a percentage to GPA</a> before treating a percentage average as a GPA.</p>
      <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/college-gpa-calculator/">Calculate GPA with grades and credits</a></p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="a-minus">
      <h2 id="a-minus">What GPA is an A-minus?</h2>
      <p><strong>An A-minus is 3.7 grade points on this site's scale.</strong> Maryland uses 3.7, while the <a href="https://catalog.ufl.edu/UGRD/academic-regulations/grades-grading-policies/">University of Florida uses 3.67</a>. Use the value on your school's official grading scale.</p>
      <p>One A-minus is a course grade, not your whole GPA. If every GPA-bearing course is an A-minus, the average stays 3.7 on the scale used here, even when course credits differ. With a mix of grades, multiply each course's points by its credits before averaging.</p>
      <p>For example, a 3-credit A and a 3-credit A-minus give (4.0 × 3 + 3.7 × 3) ÷ 6 = <strong>3.85 GPA</strong>. At a school using 3.67 for A-minus, the same two grades average 3.835 before that school's rounding or truncation rules.</p>
      <p>For several A's and one lower grade, use the <a href="/what-gpa-is-all-as-and-one-b/">A/B grade-mix tables</a>. They also cover all A-minuses and A's with a C, D or F.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="college-scale">
      <h2 id="college-scale">Do colleges use the same GPA scale?</h2>
      <p>A 4.0 maximum does not make two schools' policies identical. Compare Maryland's A-minus of 3.7 and B-plus of 3.3 with Florida's 3.67 and 3.33. Schools can also differ on A-plus, retakes, transfer work and which courses enter the GPA.</p>
      <p>Calculate <strong>GPA = total quality points ÷ total GPA credits</strong>. Quality points are a course's grade points multiplied by its credits. An A in a 4-credit course contributes 16 quality points; a B in a 2-credit course contributes 6. Together, 22 ÷ 6 = <strong>3.67</strong> when rounded to two decimals.</p>
      <p>Use your registrar's GPA credits rather than every credit on your transcript. Pass/fail courses, withdrawals and transfer credit may be handled differently. The <a href="/cumulative-gpa-calculator/">cumulative GPA calculator</a> combines semesters by their credits; simply averaging semester GPAs only works when the credit totals are equal.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="weighted-scale">
      <h2 id="weighted-scale">Weighted GPA: the 5.0 scale for AP, IB and honors</h2>
      <p>This site's weighted calculator uses a model that adds 0.5 points for honors and 1.0 for AP/IB to a passing grade. An F stays at zero. Under this model, the same A earns 4.0 in a regular course, 4.5 in honors and 5.0 in AP/IB.</p>
      <table>
        <caption class="text-left mb-3">Example weighted model used by our calculator</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Grade</th><th scope="col">Regular</th><th scope="col">Honors</th><th scope="col">AP/IB</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{gradeReference.filter(r => r.grade !== 'A+').map(({ grade, points }) => <tr><th scope="row">{grade}</th><td>{points.toFixed(1)}</td><td>{(points === 0 ? 0 : points + 0.5).toFixed(1)}</td><td>{(points === 0 ? 0 : points + 1).toFixed(1)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
      <p>High schools decide which courses receive extra weight and how much. A college may recalculate an applicant's GPA using its own rules. Do not convert a weighted GPA to unweighted by subtracting the same amount from the final average; the adjustment belongs to each eligible course.</p>
      <p>Wondering what a 4.7 or similar weighted number means on a 4.0 scale? <a href="/weighted-to-unweighted-gpa/">See why a 4.7 weighted GPA has no single unweighted answer</a>. You can also <a href="/weighted-gpa-calculator/">compare weighted and unweighted GPA</a>, or enter regular, honors and AP/IB courses in the <a href="/high-school-gpa-calculator/">high school GPA calculator</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="thresholds">
      <h2 id="thresholds">What counts as a good GPA?</h2>
      <p>Scholarships, graduation honors and academic-standing rules each have their own criteria. There is no single GPA that guarantees Dean's List, magna cum laude, admission or a scholarship at every school. Check the policy for your college, program and term rather than treating a generic threshold as a promise.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="questions">
      <h2 id="questions">Common GPA scale questions</h2>
      {faqs.map(({ q, a }) => <div><h3>{q}</h3><p>{a}</p></div>)}
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="sources">
      <h2 id="sources">Sources and calculation notes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://registrar.umd.edu/grades-records/grades/gpa-calculation">University of Maryland: GPA calculation</a>: undergraduate grade-point values and credit-weighted calculation.</li>
        <li><a href="https://catalog.ufl.edu/UGRD/academic-regulations/grades-grading-policies/">University of Florida: grades and grading policies</a>: an example of a different plus/minus scale.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Examples were calculated for this guide using the stated points and credits. Decimal percentage bands describe our calculator's convention, not a rule inferred from either university. This is an independent reference; your transcript and school's policy determine your official GPA.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  h2[id] { scroll-margin-top: 5rem; }
  table { table-layout: fixed; }
  th, td { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  tbody th { background: transparent; }
  @media (max-width: 400px) { .prose table th, .prose table td { padding: .6rem .4rem; font-size: .8rem; } }
</style>
